// `println(..)` is sugar for `<the Env in scope>.out.println(..)` and
// its value is unit -- gen_c: "a print is a statement sequence whose
// value is unit". So a body that ends in one returns NOTHING, and
// nothing does not close a function that promised an `i32`.
//
// Sema had no type for the sugar at all: no declaration of that name
// exists to resolve against, so the call fell through resolution and
// was answered with poison -- which agrees with every return type, and
// made this program legal.
//
// WITHOUT THE CLOSING `;`, ON PURPOSE. A `;`-terminated last statement
// that yields nothing is a statement, and `println("done");` stays
// legal wherever it stands. The `;` is the whole difference between a
// print and a return.
//
// position: the tail expression.

main = (env: Env) i32 {
    println("x")
}
